[Music]  in honor of women's history month we've  been talking with female leaders in  sports politics  business and entertainment this morning  we're speaking with u.s senator tammy  duff she is an  army veteran a democratic lawmaker from  illinois  and a mother of two in 2018 senator  duckworth shared a letter she wrote to  her younger self for our series  note to self but that wonderful life  won't end  so abruptly it will feel like a death  and will put the rest of your plans for  your family  for your career on life support the  helicopter pilot lost both legs when her  national guard unit was shot down over  baghdad  you'll almost die but you'll make it  just barely your survival won't have  anything to do with your own abilities  you'll make it out alive completely  because of the grit sacrifice and  outright heroism of others  you haven't done anything to be worthy  of their sacrifices  but these heroes will give you a second  chance at life  anyway  your second life begins when you wake up  a few days later  in agony non-stop unrelenting  seemingly endless agony but you will  re-emerge  this week she is releasing her first  book every day is a gift  senator duckworth good morning as we  just heard you almost died when your  helicopter was shot down you lost both  of your legs  but you have said that if you could do  it all over again you still would  why oh heck yeah  well two things the first of course are  the my my buddies the men and women i  got to serve with for all those years  my helicopter crew there's just nothing  like it they're some of the most  honorable  funny uh brilliant people i've ever met  and that includes people i've met since  i've been in the senate so  just getting to serve in my unit again  would be  priceless and plus i also love to fly  helicopters  so that would be great too senator you  grew up in  thailand uh you are biracial you face  discrimination for being half white  you were on the brink of of you were  almost below the poverty line living in  hawaii as a teenager picking up coins  for money  you talk about this experience as almost  a vaccine  preparing you for what was to come later  why is that  and how did that how did those  experiences shape you  you know i didn't really think of it  until i wrote the book  and then towards the end of the process  of writing the book i looked back and i  realized wow  all the things i described in the book  you know growing up as a kid following  my dad around  southeast asia being afraid that my dad  wouldn't come back  um when he went back to the states for a  year feeling afraid of being abandoned  all the way through to as you said you  know real extreme poverty in hawaii i  talk about selling flowers out of a  bucket by the side of the road and  you know um and picking up coins off of  the street and  uh looking back after i finished the  book i realized wow  overcoming those were real hardships for  me it was just my life  but overcoming those real hardships you  know probably set me up for being able  to face the biggest challenge i was  going to have which was recovery after  my shoot down  i know your book was so candid senator  you you say that  the hardest thing for you wasn't so much  the shoot down wasn't so much  the painful recovery it's what happens  to you in your dreams what you see in  your dreams  that is probably the most painful for  you and how it affects you and your  daughters  could you talk about that well sure i i  wanted gail to be  very upfront about everything i've been  working a lot on on  veterans healthcare and veterans mental  health and asking veterans to please get  help  um and in the book i talk about you know  if i  watch too much uh media on the iraqi  afghanistan you know wars in iraq and  afghanistan um  i actually as i described him i hope you  know i think i did a good job of it i go  to bed  in my bed in illinois and i wake up in  iraq  and i live this entire full day and i  can i remember all of the landing zones  all the lz's i remember all missions i  mean  even the mundane things that you would  have to do as part of the day  you know cleaning the windshield to your  helicopter all of i go through that  entire  you know 18-hour day what you said about  your legs  this was so fascinating to me because  you had the option of getting cosmetic  legs  which you thought that's what i want but  in the end that's not what why you took  that's not what you chose why  because they remind me of floss it's  just like you know when i would wake up  from one of these  dreams in the next day i i would have  left lived a whole life  and then i have to go through the laws i  remember oh wait that's not me now  this is me now and go through that loss  of my legs again and those cosmetic legs  are beautiful and i  there are lots of folks who who amputees  who love them and wear them but for me  they symbolize lost not where i am today  so i prefer my metal legs my titanium  ones because  those are strings senator duckworth i  want to touch on what's going on in the  country right now with asian americans  you've made the point in the book and in  recent interviews  that asian americans are in a unique  role in american culture on the one hand  they're not seen as minority enough to  deserve protection  or assistance but on the other hand  they're also forever  the other do you think that that dynamic  may be breaking  as we recognize the struggle of asian  americans right in this moment  boy i sure do hope so you know i and  that other  experience is very very real i will tell  you that i've actually been asked by  fellow americans  while i was wearing my uniform with the  american flag on my shoulder where i was  really from  like wait a minute you know in the book  i talk about this my family has been  here since before the revolution i have  ancestors who fought in the french and  indian wars and here's people asking  me where are you really from and i'm  literally wearing  the uniform of my nation with her flag  on my shoulder you had a moment with the  biden administration you said you were  offended by something they said to you  about kamala harris  well i you know i i was pushing for more  representation at the highest levels of  the biden administration  i love kamala she's my girlfriend you  know we we were freshmen in the senate  together  um i've spoken with her they actually  the  the folks in administration actually  brought up her name and said well  you have kamala you know you don't  really need any other asians in the  cabinet and i thought  wow that's really offensive you wouldn't  say we have a white male president there  should be any more white male  uh members of the cabinet why would you  say that to someone from the asian  community  so but we worked it out and then they  are i'm so pleased that they're putting  somebody in the white house west wing a  very senior  uh member of the uh you know the staff  who will be aapi will help us  navigate these issues and bring up  issues of real concern to asian  americans like the under reporting of  violence against asians  senator tammy duckworth thank you so  much the book every day as a gift goes  on sale  tomorrow we'll be right back it is good  yeah

